WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:03.900
Hey there Crazies.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.580
Do you ever wonder if we’re living in a
giant simulation?

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.600
Because I do!

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.200
Here at the Science Asylum, deep philosophical
questions are nothing new.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.620
Having clones and teleporters around brings
up all sorts of questions.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.800
But asking if the universe is a simulation
calls into question our entire existence.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.060
The movie Thirteenth Floor jumped head first
into this madness.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.200
It’s one of the deepest questions we’ve
ever covered, so let’s start a little smaller.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.120
What kind of things do we simulate and why?

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.580
Randy suggested the Three-Body Problem,
which takes us back to the days of

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:34.460
Isaac Newton.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.280
Since the invention of Newton’s laws,
we’ve been able to make amazingly accurate

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.460
predictions about
the motions of the planets and their moons,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.700
but there’s a problem here.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.420
Newton’s first and second laws of motion
only apply to one object at a time.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.600
Newton’s third law of motion and his law
of gravity only apply to two at a time.

00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:55.800
That’s great if all you’re trying to do
is model the Earth’s orbit around the Sun

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.680
or Pluto and its moon Charon.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.760
You just do some math on it and BOOYAH!

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.020
Equations of Motion.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.120
They map out any planetary path:
circular, elliptical, parabolic, or hyperbolic!

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.840
Including the Earth’s
but what if you want to see what happens to

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:09.680
the Moon?

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.020
That’s the three-body problem and it’s
drastically more complicated.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.540
So complicated, in fact, that, in 1887, Poincare
and Bruns showed it wasn’t even possible

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.340
to solve.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.540
Equations of motion are super useful.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.299
You want to know what the Earth is doing 
70 days from now?

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.860
You just plug in 70 days and BAM!

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.940
You’ve got your answer.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.099
You want to know what the Earth is doing
70,000 years from now?

00:01:30.099 --> 00:01:32.340
You just plug in 70,000 years and BAM!

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.500
You’ve got your answer.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.920
But, when there are three objects, all bets
are off and things get super weird.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.919
Here’s what the Earth and Moon’s orbit
looks like around the Sun.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.220
Well, not really, it’s exaggerated.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.820
This is what it actually looks like.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.440
It’s almost like the Moon orbits the Sun.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.920
Huh... weird...

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.880
Anyway, if you’ve got 3 objects that are
all comparable in mass,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.700
then it’s hard to find stability,
at least until one of them is thrown away.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.440
GERONIMO!

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.840
This problem isn’t limited to just gravity
either.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.859
It’s a big problem for atoms too.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.180
Hydrogen is only two objects, so no problem,
but Helium is three.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.420
Big problem!

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.700
Honestly, having only three objects isn’t
all that realistic either.

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:16.140
The solar system is made up of a Star, 8 planets,
and a bunch of moons, asteroids, and comets.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.670
Most atoms have a ton of electrons in them,
all repelling each other.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.840
It’s part of why it took us so long to figure
out the Periodic Table.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.040
It’s madness!

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.719
So how did you make all those animations if
it’s unsolvable?

00:02:26.719 --> 00:02:28.569
I simulated it with computer code!

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:32.120
See, if you’re decent at coding, you don’t
need an equation of motion.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.860
You just step the laws forward, one little
moment after the other.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.200
If you make your time-step is small enough,
the animation looks smooth.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.040
The downside is, if you want to know where
things will be and what they’ll be doing

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.409
at a certain time, you have to wait for your
simulation to get there.

00:02:46.409 --> 00:02:50.120
But doing things in real-time can get real
impractical, real fast.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.660
So you’re going to want to speed it up a
bit.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.260
And if you max out your computer, 
there are only two options:

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:54.940
One!

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.520
You can make the time-steps bigger.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.840
but you start to accumulate errors over time.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.380
WEEEE!!!!

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.580
Or two! You can get more processing power.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.920
Either by getting a better computer or by
linking computers together.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.360
Why are we talking about this?

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.960
Because this is actually how the universe
works.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.180
Even though we can sometimes use math to get
equations of motion

00:03:13.189 --> 00:03:17.090
the original laws are only defined locally
in space and time.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.440
Meaning, the universe doesn’t do any math
either.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:20.620
It lives in the moment.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:24.240
So if our universe is just a simulation, it
must have a time-step

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.460
What about Planck time!

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.459
That would make Planck time a single moment
in time

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:33.920
and Planck Length the size of a 3D pixel or
Voxel.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.480
OMG!

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.260
We’re living in a super-hi-res version of
Minecraft!

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:42.200
Planck units only have physical significance
in quantum gravity, which isn’t a thing yet.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:43.120
Dang it!

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.560
That would demand a lot of processing power
too.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:50.260
About 10 to the 120th power GHz, give or take,
which might explain why particles only have

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:51.950
definite properties when they have to.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:52.800
So, I don’t know.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.680
Do you think the universe is a simulation?

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.450
Let’s have a conversation in the comments.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.480
Thanks for liking and sharing this video
and until next time, remember,

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.680
it’s OK to be a little crazy.

